he uk government's position to continue  oil and gas extraction in the north sea  to issue new licenses is not defensible  it isn't compatible with our countries  signing the paris agreement boris  johnson is being more than negligent i  think he's being downright dangerous it  is dangerous to continue on the path  that we are on millions of lives are at  risk  you thought living through a pandemic  was bad you haven't lived through the  breakdown of your biosphere yet the uk  government doesn't see it that way  whether we export some of that oil and  gas or it's for our own use  much better to have access to that oil  and gas on our doorstep and under our  control so are you saying that the the  un and the international energy agency  are wrong when they say that new oil and  gas extraction is incompatible  with hitting global emissions targets  what i'm saying is our  the uk government's targets for net zero  by 2050 already takes into account  licensed oil and gas fields  not yet sanctioned for extraction  whatever the explanation campaigners say  this issue threatens the moral authority  of the british government  just weeks away from hosting a crucial u  n climate change summit in glasgow that  is something it can ill afford to lose  hannah thomas peter sky news in the  northeast of scotland  and if you want to see a real-time  breakdown of where electricity in great  britain is coming from just head to our  website skynews.com forward slash  climate  now in today's other climate news public  concern about climate change has reached  record levels in britain putting it  second only to the pandemic as a key  worry ipsos mori found 32 percent of  people put climate change the  environment and pollution down as a  major issue in britain double the number  of last month  five projects that will restore over 8  000 hectares of peat land across england  have been announced by the government  cornwall and yorkshire are among the  areas that will benefit from the scheme  peatlands store carbon but the uk's are  so damaged that 23 million tonnes of  carbon dioxide are released every year  and organizers have announced that the  queen will be attending the cot 26  climate conference the event is due to  take place in glasgow this november  after being postponed last year due to  the cover 19 pandemic the summit will  bring together heads of governments  business leaders activists and  scientists  extinction rebellion of course  disruption in london on a fifth day of  mass protests with activists marching  through the city of london the  environmental group says it wants banks  and institutions to stop funding fossil  fuel projects  we've heard a lot about extinction  rebellion over the past week but who are  they and what do they stand for  [Music]  if i wish to just stand here with a  placard people just walk by and nobody's  interested you have to create a  disruption to create the conversation  [Music]  so  [Music]  and on this week's episode of climate  cast we examine whether extinction  rebellions controversial tactics  have helped their cause that's out now  wherever you get your podcasts  well it is the august bank holiday  weekend and that can only mean one thing  festivals reading and leeds cream fields  all points east and carfest are just  some of the events taking place over the  next three days and with so much focus  on the climate crisis many organizers  are working to improve their  sustainability so how do festivals  impact the environment  well according to a greener festival the  average festival goer travels 144 miles  just to get there  in 2019 more than half travel to  festivals by either car or plane  traveling to and from sites tends to  make up the most of a festival's carbon  footprint accounting for eighty percent  of the sector's total emissions so what  makes up the rest well almost two  kilograms of waste per person per day is  generated by those at camping festivals  that drops to less than a kilo at  non-camping events the average festival  uses 2762  cubic meters of water almost 3 million  liters all these factors from the travel  to electricity fuel waste and water mean  the average carbon footprint for a  festival is 2  299 tons  of co2 equivalent  well with me now is claire o'neil she's  the co-founder of agreena festival which  helps events festivals and venues around  the world to become more sustainable  claire thank you so much for talking to  us so how easy is it for these events to  become more sustainable  there are some things that are  definitely easy wins but in many cases  we really depend upon the macro  environment that we exist in so for  example what are the transport networks  what are the waste facilities that we  can use  what are the energy resources that are  available the good news is that we have  been working on this subject for  decades at this stage and the  technologies are really advancing to  help festivals to be more sustainable so  for example we have  batteries that can help to supplement  generators reducing fuel we have more  discussions happening to create  installations of renewable energy such  as solar power that can then connect  back to mains or indeed connecting  festivals directly to maine's power as  well which really helps not only with  not burning fuel on site but also not  transport transporting equipment to the  site as well and is there an appetite  for these festivals for these events to  become more sustainable  unquestionably and it's from every  avenue as well we have the audiences  this is what the audiences want  all of the festival organizers we are  all individuals all of us want to be  more sustainable ultimately we can see  what's happening with our environment we  can see the problems that we have with  waste the problems that we have with  consumption of fossil fuels that are  driving the climate crisis and changing  our weather before our eyes so we have  every individual almost on board with  the project  additionally we've just recently had the  dcms from the department from the  government who deal with arts and  culture and media who are saying that we  must start to incorporate environmental  requirements into licensing for events  and these are very clear signals that we  need to implement sustainability and  have greener festivals greener events of  all types but equally it's it's a joined  up approach so we need to be giving the  right messages to the audience so that  we as an entire society can actually  improve our sustainability  is it expensive for an event to be  greener  in some ways yes because it may be that  there's upfront investments into whether  that's new technology or additional  resources of people and staff and  expertise but in the long term really  what we're looking at to be more  sustainable is to stop wasting resources  so whether that's materials whether it's  reducing the cleanup bills because  there's less waste to dispose of whether  it's reducing water reducing fuel  reducing energy ultimately that's going  to save money in the long run as well as  saving our environment and ultimately  our health  so there are upfront costs but there are  also ways that you can make big savings  today for instance by reducing how much  fuel is consumed by really knowing how  much power you need to run the stages  and the event site  okay claire thank you so much for  talking to us  it's a pleasure now in the latest in our  series of climate diaries we're hearing  from solving seven the female-led ngo  has come up with an innovative new way  to use single-use plastics which would  normally go to landfill by turning crisp  packets into school desks  [Applause]  [Music]  we learned that number 7 plastic doesn't  get collected the waste pickers will  collect a plastic bottle there'll be a  packet of crisps or an empty food  package right next to it and they will  not collect it there's no value to it  for them they have no one to sell it on  to no one buys it it doesn't get my size  holder  [Applause]  you then take the raw material and we  give it to another company that is able  to process it into planks which is then  assembled into desks and delivered to  the schools  [Music]  [Applause]  so  [Music]  children that receive a dedicated  writing surface can improve their  educational outcomes by up to 20 percent  [Music]  well that's everything from us this week  but don't forget you can watch our  digital show on snapchat and sky news  igtv in the latest episode we go behind  the scenes of our correspondent adam  parsons coverage of the devastating  floods in germany last month  anna jones will be back with the new  extended climate show from monday the  6th of september thank you for watching  [Music]

